# name: test/sql/integration/splits/expired_token.test
# description: A plan pinned to a version that has moved is refused, distinguishably
# group: [vgi_integration_splits]

# A split token names a consistency anchor — the catalog version the plan was
# pinned to. Stamping that anchor and never comparing it would make the whole
# mechanism decorative: the token would name a version and nothing would ever ask
# whether that version is still current. So redemption compares, and refuses.
#
# The refusal must be DISTINGUISHABLE from an invalid token, because only one of
# the two is worth reacting to. An expired snapshot means "re-run the query" —
# re-planning produces a valid token. A wrongly-bound or forged token means the
# opposite: re-running reproduces exactly the same token, so a connector that
# retried would burn its retry budget and fail the stage anyway. Keeping the
# anchor in the token's PLAINTEXT header rather than inside the AEAD is what
# makes the distinction expressible at all — sealed, both failures would collapse
# into one indistinguishable tag-check failure.
#
# The forged cases (a cleared seal on a keyed worker — the alg:none downgrade —
# and a mismatched bind fingerprint) are deliberately NOT tested here. They are
# unreachable through SQL by construction, because the framework owns the
# envelope and a worker cannot mint a token with a wrong fingerprint or no seal
# even on purpose. They are covered byte-for-byte instead by the shared
# cross-SDK vectors in vgi-python's tests/data/split_tokens/, which every SDK
# both parses and reproduces.

require-env VGI_TEST_WORKER

require vgi

require httpfs

statement ok
ATTACH 'example' AS example (TYPE vgi, LOCATION '${VGI_TEST_WORKER}');

# The fixture plans against a catalog version the live catalog will not agree
# with — a plan that outlived its snapshot, which is the situation this error
# exists for.
statement error
SELECT count(*) FROM example.main.split_stale_plan(n := 20, splits := 2);
----
SPLIT_SNAPSHOT_EXPIRED

# The message says what to do about it. An operator reading this should not have
# to know the protocol to learn that the query is re-runnable.
statement error
SELECT count(*) FROM example.main.split_stale_plan(n := 20, splits := 2);
----
re-run the query

# Expiry FAILS the query. It must never be papered over by silently re-planning:
# a client that re-planned mid-scan would splice rows from two different catalog
# versions into one answer, which is worse than the error.
statement error
SELECT sum(n) FROM example.main.split_stale_plan(n := 5, splits := 1);
----
SPLIT_SNAPSHOT_EXPIRED

# The catalog is unharmed — a refused token is a clean, bounded failure, not a
# poisoned connection.
query II
SELECT count(*), sum(n) FROM example.main.split_sequence(n := 40, splits := 4);
----
40	780

statement ok
DETACH example;
